{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Elohim said: Let them be gathered.</b> One needs to know whether these waters are the waters of the deep, for when He said ״יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם אֶל מָקוֹם אֶחָד״, then no other waters would remain at all, and this contradicts what the verse says (Exodus 20:4): ״וַאֲשֶׁר בַּמַּיִם מִתַּחַת לָאָרֶץ״. And if they are waters other than the deep alone, one needs to know by what means they were distinguished from the waters of the deep, to direct the utterance to them alone. Also, we have not seen that He set apart an utterance to say that such-and-such waters should be gathered, but rather unconditionally: ״יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם״ — meaning all waters found in the world. Further, why did He make the seeing of the dry land contingent upon the gathering of the waters? Further, there is a fine point to examine in the word ״וְתֵרָאֶה״, which should have said ״וְתִהְיֶה הַיַּבָּשָׁה״:" "And based on what I explained in the verse ״בְּרֵאשִׁית״ — that by the first utterance everything was created, but it was intermingled until HaShem prepared the order of each thing, each on its day — accordingly, from the words of HaShem in this utterance we know that the dry land was spread out within the waters across the width of the world, with waters above it and waters below it. And those waters that were above the dry land — the firmament divided them and bore half upon its wings, as He said <b>יְהִי רָקִיעַ בְּתוֹךְ</b>, and unqualified ״תּוֹךְ״ means the middle. And the remaining half covered the dry land, and concerning those waters HaShem said <b>יִקָּווּ אֶל מָקוֹם אֶחָד</b>, which is the Ocean, and <b>וְתֵרָאֶה הַיַּבָּשָׁה</b> in the place where it had been at the time of its creation, for there it acquired its place, except that it was covered, and He decreed upon it that it be seen." "Or it may be that the meaning of ״תֵּרָאֶה״ enters in because it was not visible in the appearance of dry things, and its hue was like the hue of the waters, with no distinction between its boundary and the boundary of the waters. And also the meaning of ״תֵּרָאֶה״ may enter in because it was fit for that which dry things ought to be, for the needs of the world." "It further intends, in His saying <b>וְתֵרָאֶה</b>, that as long as the waters are below and above the earth, the earth does not persist in its nature, for the waters would transform its nature. Go out and learn from what Rambam wrote at the beginning of Hilkhot De'ot (Hilkhot Yesodei ha-Torah, chapter 4, halakha 5), that waters transform the element of earth into water. Therefore He said ״יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם״ etc., and thereby ״תֵּרָאֶה הַיַּבָּשָׁה״ — meaning it would stand in its foundation to activate the cause of its creation. Yet its creation had already preceded in the first utterance on the first day, as was explained above. We also find ourselves saying that not all the waters of the world were gathered into the Ocean, and there are deeps beneath the earth." "And it is possible that the waters that were gathered are not distinct from the deep that is beneath the earth, and concerning that part where there is no earth, the Sages, of blessed memory (Ḥagigah 12a), said that HaShem said to the earth ״דַּי״ — that it not spread from end to end, and the waters remained joined to the lower waters." "<b>After</b> I had written all that is written, there came to my hand a midrash from Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer (chapter 5), and this is its language: On the third day the earth was a level plain like a valley, and the waters covered the face of all the earth. And when the word went forth from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, ״יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם״ etc., mountains and hills split apart across the face of all the earth, its interior rose up and rolled, and the waters gathered, and so forth — end quote. This shows that when the Holy One, blessed be He, said ״יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם,״ the earth already existed in actuality — and these are our words. Although according to the statement it appears that he explains <b>וְתֵרָאֶה הַיַּבָּשָׁה</b> to mean that the dry land would become sand as a boundary for the sea — when the waters encounter the dry land they take their place, in the manner of His saying (Jeremiah 5:22) ״אֲשֶׁר שַׂמְתִּי חוֹל גְּבוּל לַיָּם״ — and He instilled the dread of the dry land upon the sea. And the meaning of <b>וְתֵרָאֶה</b> is from the root יִרְאָה (fear), as in His saying ״הַאוֹתִי לֹא תִירָאוּ״ etc. ״אֲשֶׁר שַׂמְתִּי חוֹל גְּבוּל לַיָּם,״ and this differs from our explanation. This is the way of derash, and our way is the way of peshat, and the verse may intend all." "<b>And the Ramban</b>, of blessed memory, explained the saying of <b>וְתֵרָאֶה הַיַּבָּשָׁה</b> to mean that the earth was mixed with the waters, and HaShem decreed that they separate one from the other and the aspect of earth consolidate and become dry land — end quote. And his explanation, of blessed memory, requires sweetening. Also, the plain sense of the verse does not indicate this. Also, according to his words, of blessed memory, there would have been no need for the waters to gather to one place; rather, He should have said to the dry land to appear in the order described in the Rabbi's words — that the earth clarify itself from the water and float upward, and the waters remain in their place. Further, from the verse it implies that all the waters found beneath the firmament that were adjacent to the earth gathered to one place, but this is not implied according to the Rabbi's explanation. Further, the expression ״וְתֵרָאֶה״ is not precise according to his way, of blessed memory. And what is correct in my eyes is as I have explained: that the earth lay spread out within the waters, with water above and water below it, and concerning the waters above the earth the Decreeing One decreed that the firmament be divided within them and bear the upper waters in its wings, and the portion remaining beneath the firmament would not remain evenly under all the firmament but would gather in one place — and this is His saying <b>יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם מִתַּחַת הַשָּׁמַיִם אֶל מָקוֹם אֶחָד</b>, and thereby <b>תֵרָאֶה הַיַּבָּשָׁה</b> according to its plain sense." "And it appears to say that all aspects of the waters that are upon the earth — both the upper ones above the firmament and those that gathered to one place — are in the aspect of male waters. And therefore you will find that the generation of seeds comes from the waters of the Ocean, which are the gathered waters, as they said, of blessed memory (Ta'anit 9b), that from the waters of the Ocean come the rains, and after HaShem rains from them, [waters] rise from the deep to meet them. And this is their saying, of blessed memory (Ta'anit 25b): ״No drop descends from above unless two drops rise to meet it from below״ — and seeds do not sprout, neither from the rain[-waters] nor from the deep waters, for male alone or female alone do not reproduce. And here are their words, of blessed memory, who said (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer, ch. 23) that there are two waters, one male and one female, and this is their language: ״And the waters prevailed exceedingly״ — when the male waters joined with the female waters, they prevailed all the more."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {7 "The closing quotation from Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer appears to cite ״וְהַמַּיִם גָּבְרוּ מְאֹד״ (Genesis 7:19), continuing the gendered-waters theme; translated hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:54:52.257100112Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}